    <!-- 1. What Is a Screenshot Checker? -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        What Is a Screenshot Checker?
      </h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>screenshot checker</strong> is a specialized software tool that inspects screenshots and images for underlying evidence, readable text, metadata, and potential signals of editing. Rather than treating an image as a simple flat picture, a screenshot checker investigates structural elements inside—including font alignment, typography consistency, compression boundaries, and embedded technical data.
      </p>
      <p>
        People use a <strong>screenshot checker online</strong> for everyday situations: an urgent SMS warning that an account is locked, a transaction slip for a money transfer, or a captured social media thread. Because screenshots can be created quickly, cropped easily, or edited with basic graphics software, they can sometimes present misleading or selective representations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Checking digital evidence matters because a static capture only shows what was displayed on a screen at one moment. Evaluating visible content, structural patterns, and metadata together helps you make informed choices before reacting to urgent requests or making financial decisions.
      </p>
    </section>

