    <!-- 4. Can a Screenshot Checker Detect Fake Screenshots? -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Can a Screenshot Checker Detect Fake Screenshots?
      </h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>fake screenshot checker</strong> can identify signals that may indicate manipulation, but no automated software can guarantee that every screenshot is authentic or counterfeit.
      </p>
      <p>
        When screenshots are edited, they often contain subtle inconsistencies:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-sm pl-1">
        <li><strong>Inconsistent Fonts and Weights:</strong> Spliced text often exhibits mismatched typefaces, unnatural letter spacing, or uneven anti-aliasing.</li>
        <li><strong>Unusual Alignment and Spacing:</strong> Inserted text or numbers may align poorly with surrounding UI borders.</li>
        <li><strong>Localized Compression Inconsistencies:</strong> Modifying a portion of an image and resaving can produce varying JPEG compression rates between modified and original regions.</li>
        <li><strong>Contradictory Metadata:</strong> Internal EXIF tags or editor software fingerprints may contradict visible timestamps.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        ScreenshotChecker does not claim to always tell if a screenshot is fake. Instead, it identifies signals that may indicate manipulation and helps you investigate the image further.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Screenshot Verification: Why Context Matters -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Screenshot Verification: Why Context Matters
      </h2>
      <p>
        When performing <strong>screenshot verification</strong>, remember that a screenshot is a visual representation of information rather than the original source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Screenshots are commonly shared as proof of bank transfers, WhatsApp chats, emails, delivery updates, and social media posts. However, visual interfaces can easily be simulated through browser developer tools, templates, or mockup software.
      </p>
      <p>
        For critical transactions, visual evidence alone is never conclusive. <strong>Always verify the transaction in your own bank or payment application rather than relying only on a screenshot.</strong> A <strong>screenshot authenticity checker</strong> is a valuable inspection aid, but verifying the original source remains essential.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. Can You Check a Fake Payment Screenshot? -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Can You Check a Fake Payment Screenshot?
      </h2>
      <p>
        Fake payment confirmations are widely used in online transaction scams. Deceptive actors create fake UPI receipts, altered bank confirmations, and forged transfer IDs to claim money has been sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        With our <a href="/payment-screenshot-checker" class="text-primary underline hover:text-primary-active">Payment Screenshot Review</a> tool, you can check transaction captures for:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-sm pl-1">
        <li>Digit alignment anomalies around currency amounts and fees.</li>
        <li>Inconsistent font weights on sender and recipient fields.</li>
        <li>Compression artifacts around edited timestamps and transaction IDs.</li>
        <li>Exposed financial data that should be redacted before sharing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Keep in mind: <em>A screenshot can show what an image contains, but only the actual payment provider or recipient account can confirm whether money was received.</em> Never release items before verifying settled balances in your account.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7. Can Screenshots Be Used for Fraud? -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Can Screenshots Be Used for Fraud?
      </h2>
      <p>
        Screenshots are frequently used to support online fraud and social engineering tactics:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-sm pl-1">
        <li><strong>Fake Bank Messages:</strong> SMS captures claiming cards are frozen to provoke urgent phone calls.</li>
        <li><strong>Fake KYC Warnings:</strong> Deceptive notices threatening immediate account suspension.</li>
        <li><strong>Fake Delivery Updates:</strong> Courier messages demanding address confirmation or unpaid customs fees.</li>
        <li><strong>Fake Rewards and Lotteries:</strong> Unsolicited vouchers prompting login credentials.</li>
        <li><strong>Fake Job Offers:</strong> Captures promising guaranteed returns or remote employment.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To inspect suspicious messages for dangerous triggers and malicious URLs, use our <a href="/screenshot-scam-checker" class="text-primary underline hover:text-primary-active">Scam & Fraud Checker</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 8. Can AI-Generated Images and Screenshots Be Detected? -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Can AI-Generated Images and Screenshots Be Detected?
      </h2>
      <p>
        With modern generative models, synthetic graphics and AI-edited screenshots are increasingly common. Our <a href="/ai-image-detector" class="text-primary underline hover:text-primary-active">AI Image Detector</a> helps users analyze visual files for synthetic indicators.
      </p>
      <p>
        Key signals analyzed include:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-sm pl-1">
        <li><strong>Synthetic Visual Signals:</strong> Unnatural pixel noise and synthetic smoothing patterns.</li>
        <li><strong>Unusual Geometry:</strong> Inconsistent line geometry and distorted interface components.</li>
        <li><strong>Text Rendering Artifacts:</strong> Warped lettering and irregular character forms.</li>
        <li><strong>C2PA Content Credentials:</strong> Embedded cryptographic metadata where present.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because AI tools continually evolve, automated detection does not offer 100% certainty. It serves as a helpful clue alongside human evaluation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 9. Why Screenshot Analysis Is Not Always 100% Certain -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink font-normal tracking-tight">
        Why Screenshot Analysis Is Not Always 100% Certain
      </h2>
      <p>
        Transparent security analysis requires recognizing realistic constraints:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-sm pl-1">
        <li><strong>Recompression and Resizing:</strong> Messaging apps like WhatsApp and social platforms recompress images and strip original metadata.</li>
        <li><strong>Legitimate Editing:</strong> Cropping or highlighting text with annotations creates localized pixel changes that resemble tampering.</li>
        <li><strong>Image Resolution:</strong> Lower quality files provide fewer visual details for forensic and OCR analysis.</li>
        <li><strong>No Single Signal Proves Authenticity:</strong> Reliable verification requires evaluating multiple evidence points together.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        ScreenshotChecker is designed as an <strong>investigation aid</strong> to help you explore visible data, text, and metadata responsibly.
      </p>
    </section>
